A||||5||BENGAL4||HELP. terryk, Difference between OBD I & OBD II. ||||5||
Z||000000||BENGAL4||04-19-2001||11:55 PM||||Terry,<P>Would you explain to me the difference between the 2? As you know, the OBDII has not worked on my swap. They are now wiring it to an OBDI computer. I was also told, that the car will now run at peak performance. Why didn't OBDII work?<P>Thanks<P>Bill||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jay||04-20-2001||10:23 AM||||What swap are you doing?  I had the same problem with my conversion (Northstar).  I had to convert the engine from a 97 to a 95 by changing throttle body, TPS, and O2 sensors.  I'm not exactly sure but it has to do with how the computer processes and recieves information from the various moduels and sensors.  It is harder to trick an OBD2 system.  Anyone have a more detailed explination?<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1988LRC||04-20-2001||11:01 AM||||when I worked for Toyota the easy way to explain  OBD1 is that with a jumper wire or paper clip the computer will give you the codes OBD2 requires special equipment "break out box" that interfaces with the computer and goes through all systems/sensors then tells you what is wrong much more accurate but without the scan tools you're SOL||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||04-20-2001||12:02 PM||||The difference I've seen in the code are that OBDI was the first attempt to start predicting emissions controls degradation. Losing the paper clip code flash is a side effect difference. OBDII added tons more checks for the potential failure and merged towards an industry standard on error reporting, checking, and added flight recorders. 94/95 3800 code has the flight recorder tagged on. Without getting more boring, that's the jest of it.<P>The problems people have with OBDII swaps is not really OBDII issues as much as the fact that the OBDII PCM's allowed more integration between the boxes (PCM/BCM/ABS, etc). The processor has more time available to talk back and forth and make the car's operation smarter. <P>This little benefit makes disabling stuff harder without changing the code. In addition, The Fed's started applying a lot of pressure to the car manufacturer's to make hacking the code harder. You can't make it impossible, but the flash PCM's are more difficult to hack.  If you've ever seen the 96+GTP code vs to 92-95 3800 code, you'd see what I mean. <P>How about giving me more details on what you ran into. What trans? We've been running two Series II engines on the 94/95 code.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||04-20-2001||12:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B> Without getting more boring, that's the jest of it.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Get boring on us, terryk. We have long attention spans.||||reg||1
